[2026-08-24 11:02:07] [cot:thought] The post will contrast
[2026-08-24 11:02:07] [cot:thought] two angles. National CPI compares daycare inflation against average hourly earnings. Metro price levels will show which cities price out median earners.
[2026-08-24 11:02:07] [cot:thought] From 2018 to mid-2026, childcare CPI rose 34.7% while CES wages grew 39.2%. Wages still outpaced costs nationally.
[2026-08-24 11:02:07] [cot:thought] Child Care Aware reports
[2026-08-24 11:02:07] [cot:thought] that childcare prices as a share of income remain elevated, at 10% for dual-income couples and 33% for single parents. Metro infant care prices exceed the HHS 7% threshold of median household income everywhere.
[2026-08-24 11:02:07] [cot:thought] The analysis will also examine usual weekly earnings of parents with children, real wages for lower quartiles, and CES leisure and hospitality rates.
[2026-08-24 11:02:07] [cot:thought] A stronger framing draws from HHS OPRE market rate studies, which show listed prices rising faster than subsidy reimbursement.
[2026-08-24 11:02:07] [cot:thought] BLS figures reveal
[2026-08-24 11:02:07] [cot:thought] three distinct CPI windows. 2014???2019 saw daycare and average hourly earnings rise neck-and-neck at roughly 14.5% and 14.3%. 2022???2025 gave childcare a slight edge at 16.4% versus 15.0% for wages. 2023???2025 shows a sharper divergence: childcare averaged about 5.5% year-over-year while CES wages averaged only 4%.
[2026-08-24 11:02:07] [cot:thought] The headline will
[2026-08-24 11:02:07] [cot:thought] state that since 2022, day-care CPI has outpaced private average hourly earnings by roughly 1.4 percentage points cumulatively. Center infant care in high-price metros still consumes 15???27% of median household income, well above the 7% HHS affordability benchmark. A metro panel will estimate these shares using Child Care Aware and state fact sheets, clearly marking them as estimates.
